NAME
    disasm - the YACC1 disassembler: machine code back to source

SYNOPSIS
    disasm [-s] FILE [START [COUNT]]
    disasm [-s] -m ADDR [COUNT]

DESCRIPTION
    Shows machine code as instructions, one a line:

        AAAA  bb bb bb  MNEMONIC OPERANDS
        C000  19 CF FF  MVIW R1,0CFFFH
        C003  0E 05     LDAI 05H

    the address, the instruction's bytes, then the instruction in the
    assembler's own dialect (asm, asmc and RC/asm on the Mac): numbers
    in hex with an H and a 0 before a letter (05H, 0FFH, 0F000H),
    registers R0..R7, ports P0..PF. Every line assembles back to the
    same bytes.

    FILE is a program file shown as if loaded: the addresses start at
    the load address in its directory entry (where load and run put
    it; /BIN programs load at 5000), or at START, and go to the end of
    the file or for COUNT bytes. The file is read, not loaded, so
    disasm can show any program, itself included (disasm /BIN/DISASM).

    -m shows memory from ADDR instead, COUNT bytes (default 40): the
    ROM monitor (disasm -m F000 20), the OS (1000), a program you
    loaded (load it, then disasm -m its address).

    START, ADDR and COUNT are hex. The instructions shown are the ones
    that START inside the range; the last is completed past its end
    while there are bytes (to the file's end; up to FFFF in memory).

    A byte that begins no instruction is shown as a DB line (DB 00H):
    a byte that is no opcode (00 A5 AE F8 F9 FA FF), an instruction
    whose operand byte cannot be (JSRUR with a register byte over 7),
    or an instruction cut off by the end of the file. Data between the
    code comes out as instructions or DBs; either way it assembles back
    to the same bytes. disasm does not follow jumps or tell code from
    data: it decodes from where it starts.

    The output is ordinary output, so > and | work (disasm F | more,
    disasm F > F.LST); errors go to the screen: "disasm: not found:
    F", "disasm: bad address: X", "disasm: bad count: X", "disasm:
    START is not in the file: X". disasm is /BIN/DISASM.

OPTIONS
    -s    source only: an ORG line, then the instructions indented,
          without addresses and bytes - a file asm takes back
    -m    memory instead of a file

EXAMPLES
    disasm /BIN/HELLO           the first /BIN program, all of it
    disasm /BIN/HELLO 5010 10   16 bytes of it from 5010
    disasm -m F000 20           the ROM monitor's first instructions
    disasm -m FFC0 40           the ROM's BIOS vectors
    disasm /BIN/HELLO | more    a page at a time
    disasm -s P > P.ASM         then asm P.ASM Q: Q is P again (cmp P Q)

NOTES
    The instruction table is generated from yacc1.def, the file the
    assemblers' tables come from (tools/gen_y1_distab.py), so disasm
    knows every instruction they know - the 2026-09-24 LDZ, STZ,
    ADDIW and SHL16 and BRUR among them - and cannot disagree with
    them. Registers R8 and R9, which RC/asm accepts, are not
    registers of the machine: their bytes read as other instructions.
    New for the YACC1 2026-09-29 (the P8X disasm decodes the P8X's
    instructions; only its idea of a table generated from the
    assembler's is kept).

